 What the desk pane hands the browser when the pointer is clicked, measured
 without a pointer. `GopBrowser` calls `browser-handle-mouse-click` with the
 pump's coordinates; this pins what that function does with them.

 It is measured here rather than through the desk because THE DESK'S CURSOR
 DOES NOT TRACK SCRIPTED MOUSE INPUT IN THIS BED. Measured 2026-08-11: with
 plain `-mouse` the USB mouse does not enumerate at all (`um-ok` false), and
 with `-hid-combo` it enumerates and delivers reports -- a probe polling
 `mouse-pump` in a tight loop read the scripted position back within 640
 exits -- yet the desktop's cursor stays at the `mouse-warp` position
 `desk-run` gives it, both on the desktop and inside the browser pane. That
 is a pre-existing property of the bed and it applies to `desk-loop`'s own
 click handling exactly as it applies to the pane, which is why the desk was
 developed against real hardware. Do not read a passing run here as evidence
 that clicking works on metal.

 The rows scan DOWN the window at a fixed x, so the output says which band
 does what rather than asserting one hand-picked coordinate. The addresses
 are what the browser believes is loaded after the click.

Section: Test

  addr-of : BrowserState -> Text
  addr-of (st) =
    when browser-active-tab st
      is Just (t) -> t.tab-address
      is None -> "(none)"

  click : BrowserState, Integer, Integer -> BrowserState
  click (st) (mx) (my) = browser-handle-mouse-click st mx my

 Each row builds its OWN state, and that is not waste. `__record-set` stores
 in place and returns the same record, so a click that adds a tab mutates the
 state the caller still holds: a first version of this test threaded one
 state through every row and read `tabs=2` on all six, because row one's new
 tab was still there when row two ran. Sharing the subject between rows
 measures the rows in order rather than the rows themselves.

  row : Integer, Integer -> Text
  row (mx) (my) =
    let st = browser-init 1024 768 browser-theme-default display-init-text-fallback
    in let after = click st mx my
    in integer-to-text my & " tabs=" & integer-to-text (list-length after.bs-tabs) & " " & addr-of after

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (row 200 10)
    print-line-uni (row 200 45)
    print-line-uni (row 200 100)
    print-line-uni (row 200 160)
    print-line-uni (row 200 240)
    print-line-uni (row 200 320)
    print-line-uni (row 10 45)
    print-line-uni (row 60 10)
  end
